# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for the stdcxx revival
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them. The =analysis-= notes under =doc/notes= carry the
longer investigations.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* Library

The library itself, =include= and =src=: its behaviour, its interface
and what it carries that it no longer uses. The locale entries come
first, the easiest at the top: fixes of a line or two, then fixes that
take work, then the entries that wait on a decision; what lies outside
the locale is last.

** TODO =%%= in a =time_get= pattern always fails

The parser's table in =src/time_put.cpp= (=__rw_get_timepunct=, the
overload at 1003-1385) has =case '%': break;= with no count and no
member, so =do_get= sets =failbit= at =include/loc/_time_get.cc= 928:
=get= with "%d%%" on "50%" fails where it should store 50. Since the
2005 import. Found by probe, 2026-11-02, round 2 of the locale
document.

Fix: =%%= matches one =%= of the input.

Check: the row "%d%%" on "50%", mday 50, goodbit.

** TODO =%F= is written and counted but never read

=time_put= writes =%F= and =do_date_order= counts it, but the parser's
table has no ='F'=, so =do_get= fails it. =nan_TW.UTF-8@latin= has
=d_fmt= "%F", so its =get_date= fails on its own =%x= ("2026-11-02"
gives =failbit=), against 14882:2003 22.2.5.1.2 p4. Found by probe,
2026-11-02. (=analysis-time-get-state.md='s directives table lists
=%F= among the fixed formats read; it is not.)

Fix: =%F= in the parser's table as =%Y-%m-%d=, the way =%D= is =%m/%d/%y=.

Check: the =%x= round trip under =nan_TW.UTF-8@latin=; a row "%F" on
"2026-11-02".

** TODO A pattern that ends inside a conversion succeeds

"%Y%" and "%Y%E" on "2026x" store 2026 with =goodbit=; N3242
22.4.5.1.1 p8 asks for =failbit= on a pattern that is not a valid
sequence of directives. =get= (=include/loc/_time_get.cc=, loop at
483, =__fmt [0]= set at 506) never checks a pending =%= after the
loop. Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: a =%= or =%E= or =%O= left pending at the pattern's end sets
=failbit=.

Check: the two rows, =failbit=, nothing stored.

** TODO The primary =codecvt= returns without setting the next pointers

=include/loc/_codecvt.h= 82-112: the primary template's =do_in=,
=do_out= and =do_unshift= return =noconv= or =error= without setting
=from_next= and =to_next= (22.2.1.5.2 p2 wants ~to_next == to~ on
=noconv=), and =do_length= returns =INT_MAX=, beyond what p10 allows.
By reading, 2026-11-02.

Fix: set the next pointers to the from and to arguments; =do_length=
returns the lesser of =max= and the input length.

Check: a probe on =codecvt<unsigned char, unsigned char, mbstate_t>=.

** TODO The caller's =iostate= survives a =time_get= parse

Every exit of the =time_get= members ORs into =err=
(=include/loc/_time_get.cc= 75, 92, 336, 531, 575, 658, 848, 967);
14882:2003 22.2 p2 says the facets ignore the value they are given,
and N3242 p8 and p12 start from =goodbit=. Passing =failbit= in, "%Y"
on "2026" returns =eofbit|failbit= and still stores the year. Since
2005. =num_get= assigns =goodbit= first (=_num_get.cc= 111, 157);
=money_get= ORs too (=_money_get.cc= 502). Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: assign, as =num_get= does, in =time_get= and =money_get=.

Check: a row passing =failbit= in, returning the parse's own state;
=22.locale.time.get= and =money.get= on their pins otherwise.

** TODO A failed codecvt mapping publishes the file's size with no data

=__rw_mmap= in =src/mman.cpp= writes =*size= from =stat= before it
opens and maps the file, so a mapping that fails after =stat= hands
back a null pointer and a non-zero size. =__rw_facet::_C_get_data=
guards the general path: a null result stores the no-database marker,
=_C_impsize= of =size_t (-1)=, beside the null pointer
(=src/facet.cpp= 248-254). The wide =codecvt_byname= path does not:
it stores the size the mapper wrote beside the null pointer
(=src/facet.cpp= 297-301). Every later call takes the C library path,
which is the no-database behaviour by another route, and the
destructor is guarded by the pointer; the seam is a facet whose size
says it holds data it does not. Recorded in
=doc/notes/analysis-facet-first-use.md= chapter 2.1 on 2026-09-28,
never queued.

Fix: =__rw_mmap= writes =*size= only once the mapping exists; the
codecvt path stores =size_t (-1)= for a null result, as the general
path does.

Check: a probe that makes the codecvt database of a byname locale
unreadable (mode 000) under 15D: the facet converts through the C
library, =_C_impsize= is =size_t (-1)=, and a second call does not
map again.

** TODO =length= under a =@UCS= name walks the wrong trie

=__rw_libstd_do_length= (=src/wcodecvt.cpp= 823) walks
=utf8_to_ext=, keyed by the UTF-8 of the wide value, under any UCS
flag, where =do_in= walks =n_to_w=; since e6c7726b. Installed in a
locale, =de_DE.ISO-8859-1@UCS= gives =length ("a\xe9")= 1 where =in=
converts 2 bytes, and =ja_JP.EUC-JP@UCS= =length ("a\xa4\xa2")= 1.
Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: =length= walks the trie =in= walks.

Check: =length= equals the bytes =in= consumes under both locales
(rows in =22.locale.codecvt=).

** TODO A split character through the C library loses its state

On =mbrtowc='s -2, =__rw_libc_do_in= (=src/wcodecvt.cpp= 249-250,
e6a99a20) returns =ok= with nothing consumed, but =mbrtowc= has
already absorbed the bytes into the state: under =de_DE.utf8=
=in ("\xc3")= returns =ok=, consumed 0, =mbsinit= false; the next
call asserts (1201) in a debug build and returns =error= otherwise.
A C-library-backed wide stream that splits a character across
buffers breaks here. Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: return =partial= with the bytes consumed and the state kept, as
22.2.1.5.2 p3 describes.

Check: =in= of the two halves of U+00E9 across two calls yields U+00E9;
a =wifstream= row reading a UTF-8 file through a 1-byte buffer.

** TODO =IS_UTF8= and =IS_UTF16*= can never be true

=src/wcodecvt.cpp= 110-113 compare =UCS_TYPE (x)=, which masks with
=__rw_ucs_mask= (89: the three UCS bits), against =__rw_utf8= and the
UTF-16 flags (81 ff., outside that mask), so the four tests are
always false. =do_max_length= (1558) returns 0 for =UTF-8= and
=utf-8=. Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: mask with =__rw_utf_mask= (95) in the four tests, then read
every site that relied on their being false.

Check: =max_length ()= of the UTF-8 facet returns 6; =22.locale.codecvt=
on its pin, plus a row for =max_length= of the UTF-8 facet.

** TODO =ctype_byname<wchar_t>= builds three tables nothing reads

The C-library fallback of =ctype_byname<wchar_t>= (=src/wctype.cpp=
569-640) builds three 256-entry tables; every virtual is overridden,
so nothing reads them. By reading, 2026-11-02.

Fix: drop the tables, or the overrides that make them dead.

Check: reading; =22.locale.ctype*= on their pins.

** TODO =collate::do_transform= leaks a codecvt mapping per call

=src/collate.cpp= 878-884 (narrow) and 1053-1059 (wide): a transform
that meets a character outside the table maps the codeset's codecvt
database for its validity check through =__rw_get_facet_data= and
never releases it. Under =zh_CN.UTF-8=, 100 wide transforms of U+743B
add 100 mappings (=/proc/self/maps=). Since the 2005 import. Found by
probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: release the mapping at the end of the call, or map once per
facet.

Check: the mapping count stays flat over 100 transforms.

** TODO Category 0 is read as =LC_CTYPE=

=locale::collate & locale::ctype= is 0, and glibc's =LC_CTYPE= is 0, so
=_C_LC2category= (=src/locale_body.cpp= 589) maps the empty category to
=ctype=: =locale (classic, "de_DE.UTF-8", collate & ctype)= takes de's
=LC_CTYPE=, and so does the two-locale constructor. 14882:2003
22.1.1.1.1 p1. Found by probe, 2026-11-02, round 2 of the locale
document.

Fix: a category of 0 selects nothing; the LC_ mapping takes only the
LC_ constants.

Check: that locale's =name ()= is "C" and its =ctype<char>= is the
classic one.

** TODO The category constructors drop the first locale's user facets

=_C_combine (const __rw_locale&, int)= (=src/locale_combine.cpp=
431-432) takes the managed branch because =_C_is_managed (cat)= reads
only the standard bitmaps. With ~a = locale (locale ("de_DE.UTF-8"), &f)~,
both =locale (a, "en_US.UTF-8", locale::numeric)= and the two-locale
form come out named and =has_facet<F>= is false on them. 14882:2003
22.1.1.2 p9, p11, p14, p15. Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: a locale holding user facets is not managed for any category.

Check: =has_facet<F>= true and =name ()= "*" on both results.

** TODO ~operator==~ ignores the standard facets when both locales hold user facets

=src/locale_eq.cpp= 118-160: ~locale (de, &f) == locale (locale (de, &g), &f)~
is true with =g= a program =ctype<char>=, so two locales with
different =ctype<char>= facets compare equal. 14882:2003 22.1.1.4 p1.
Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: compare every slot, standard and user.

Check: the probe prints 0.

** TODO Dead code in the locale class and the catalog

=src/locale_body.h= 131-146: two name-taking =__rw_locale=
constructors with no callers, whose temporary would trip the
~0 == _C_ref~ assertion of the destructor in a debug build.
=src/locale_body.cpp= 377-485: an =#if 0= block in =__rw_expand_name=.
=src/catalog.cpp= and =src/catalog.h= compile to nothing on every
supported target: =_RWSTD_NO_CATOPEN_CATGETS=, tested at
=src/messages.cpp= 43, is defined by no probe. Found by reading,
2026-11-02.

Fix: remove, or characterize =catopen= and keep the catalog under it.

Check: grep, then a 15D build with the code removed.

** TODO =num_put= writes an unbounded run of zeros for a negative precision

=src/num_put.cpp= 773-825: =fixed=, =setprecision (-1000)=, 1234
fires the assertion at 837 after the write. Found by probe,
2026-11-02.

Fix: bound the run by the buffer, growing it as the digits path does.

Check: AddressSanitizer on the case.

** TODO =num_put= overruns its buffer when it groups

=__rw_put_groups= (=src/num_put.cpp= 387-393) ignores =bufsize=
(=_RWSTD_UNUSED=) and writes the separators in place; =_C_put='s
stack array is 308 bytes and a grown heap block is exact-size.
=fixed << 1e300= under =en_US.UTF-8= ends in "malloc(): corrupted top
size"; a user grouping of "\3" fires the assertion ~len <= bufsize~
(837) after the overrun. Found by probe, 2026-11-02, round 2 of the
locale document.

Fix: =__rw_put_groups= grows the buffer, or the caller sizes it for
the separators before the call.

Check: both cases under AddressSanitizer; a row in =22.locale.num.put=
for =fixed= 1e300 grouped.

** TODO =money_put= copies a digit string into its buffer without a bound

=include/loc/_money_put.cc= 391: the digits are copied into the
304-byte =__buf= unbounded when grouping is non-empty; 1000 digits
under =en_US.UTF-8= give SIGSEGV. Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: size the buffer for the digits and the separators.

Check: AddressSanitizer on the case.

** TODO =money_put= smashes the stack on a long double past its buffer

=include/loc/_money_put.cc= 320-337: after =__rw_put_num= moves the
digits to the heap, =do_put= still widens =__n= characters from the
304-byte stack =__buf= into the 304-element =__wbuf=; the heap block
leaks too. =put (..., 1e320L)= gives "stack smashing detected". Found
by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: widen from the buffer =__rw_put_num= returned, into one sized
for it, and free it.

Check: AddressSanitizer; a row in =22.locale.money.put= for 1e320L.

** TODO =__rw_put_groups= stores a group size in a =char=

In its =no_groups= mode (=src/num_put.cpp=, called from
=money_put::do_put= for a string) =__rw_put_groups= writes one byte
per output group, the group's digit count, for the inline separator
walk of =include/loc/_money_put.cc= to read. A leftmost group of 256
or more digits wraps: 256 becomes 0 and ends the walk, 300 becomes 44
and misplaces a separator. Only an unlimited leftmost group reaches
it, that is a grouping element of 0, negative or =CHAR_MAX=.
Hypothesis from reading, 2026-11-02.

Fix: a wider element, or the walk driven by the grouping string.

Check: =money_put= of a 300-digit string under grouping "\3\0",
counting the separators.

** TODO Two latent overruns in the time facets

=include/loc/_time_get.cc= 545 (=__names [100]=) and 145
(=__fmts [100]=) take every era record, and localedef caps =alt_digits=
at 100 but not eras (=util/localedef/scalar.cpp= 473-505).
=__rw_fmt_time= keeps one =bufsize= while it advances
(=src/time_put.cpp= 1506/1517, wide 1643/1654), so an expansion past
256 elements overruns =do_put='s =__buf [256]=
(=include/loc/_time_put.cc= 75). Shipped data reaches neither: 11 eras
at most (ja_JP), 172 bytes for the longest =%c= (km_KH.UTF-8). Found by
reading, 2026-11-02.

Fix: bound the era count where the arrays are filled (or size them
from the record); carry the remaining size through =__rw_fmt_time=.

Check: generated locales with 101 era records and a 300-character
=%c=, under the sanitizer.

** TODO Racing =locale::global= calls can split the C and C++ locales

=locale::global= (=src/locale_global.cpp= 60-72) calls =setlocale= and
swaps the body under two different locks, so two racing calls can
leave the C locale from one and the C++ global from the other.
Hypothesis, by reading, 2026-11-02.

Fix: one lock around both, or a decision that the race is the
caller's.

Check: a =22.locale.statics.mt= variant comparing =setlocale (LC_ALL, 0)=
with =locale ().name ()= after the race.

** TODO =%EY= keeps a year its format then fails on

=__rw_read_era= stores its local =tm= whenever no bit but =eofbit= is
set (=include/loc/_time_get.cc= 301-334). Under =%EY= a =%Ey= of the
era format that succeeds sets the year in the local =tm=; a literal
that then fails, or the end of input, adds only =eofbit=, so the year
read is stored: "Heisei 8 Nex" under the test locale stores 1996 with
=eofbit=. The comment at =include/loc/_time_get.cc= 99-100 and
=doc/notes/analysis-time-get-state.md= 9.2 say a failed era directive
leaves the year as it was; =%EC= and a lone =%Ey= do (=src/era.cpp=
340-373). Found by the documentation sweep of 2026-11-02, by reading.

Fix: a decision: keep the year a =%EY= read before its format failed
(C++11 22.4.5.1.2 p13 leaves the members unspecified), or store
nothing unless the whole format matched. The comment at =include/loc/_time_get.cc= 97-100 and
=analysis-time-get-state.md= 9.2 follow.

Check: a row under the test locale, "Heisei 8 Nex" against =%EY=,
pinning the decision either way.

** TODO =eofbit= before the pattern ends sets =failbit= and stores nothing

"%Y-%m" and "%Y " on "2026" both fail: the walk ends with =failbit=
when a directive sets =eofbit= before the pattern is done. N3242 p8
lists ~err != goodbit~ before ~s == end~, a reading under which
=eofbit= is left alone and the year stored. Found by probe,
2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision on how p8's order reads; then the walk.

Check: the two rows pinning the reading taken.

** TODO Implied =tm_wday= and =tm_yday= are left as the caller had them

"%Y %m %d" determines the weekday and the day of the year; the
library stores neither and glibc's =strptime= fills both. Not a
conformance question (N3242 p12 owes only the directive's members);
a decision on whether to fill them. Found 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision; if filled, after the walk, from the three fields.

Check: a row "%Y %m %d" on "2026-11-02" with =tm_wday= 0, =tm_yday= 305.

** TODO =%C= of a year past 9999 writes the century modulo 100

Year 10000 writes "00", pinned at =tests/localization/22.locale.time.put.cpp=
708; the POSIX.1-2024 width sentence b54acca9 cites gives "100".
Found 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision, then the row.

Check: the row at 708 says what the decision says.

** TODO =put= over a pattern pads every directive to the width

The width of the stream is applied to every directive of a pattern
and never reset: "%H:%M" at width 5 writes "***09:***05". No row has
a pattern with a width. Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision: the width applies once (the first directive, as
=num_put= consumes it) or to each; then reset or keep.

Check: a row with a width and a two-directive pattern.

** TODO The blank bit is visible through =ctype<char>::is= under a database

=plan-localedef-backend.md= 3.1 says the =blank= bit written as 0x400
is "invisible until a =ctype_base::blank= names it", and the header
comment of =util/localedef/wctype.cpp= says it "stays unread". By
probe, under =de_DE.ISO-8859-1= the vector =ctype<char>::is= returns
0x403 for a space (the classic facet 0x003) and =table ()= hands out
the same words; the wide vector form strips it with =__rw_all=. Found
2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision: strip the bit in the narrow vector form and =table ()=
as the wide form does, or name it a visible extension and record it.

Check: the vector =is= of a space under a database locale equals the
classic facet's, or the extension is in the master document.

** TODO Database locales have empty =truename= and =falsename=

=util/localedef/wnumeric.cpp= 94-97 writes both names empty, so under
every database locale =boolalpha= writes nothing and reads nothing
(en_US, de_DE, fr_FR by probe); the C-library path gives "true" and
"false". =tests/localization/22.locale.numpunct.cpp= 223 carries
"FIXME: exercise falsename() and truename() in named locales". Found
2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision on a named locale's names (the C facet's, as the
C-library path does, is the natural one); the writer and a test row
follow.

Check: a =boolalpha= round trip under =en_US.UTF-8= from the database.

** TODO A multibyte separator in a narrow facet comes back as its first byte

A narrow =numpunct= or =moneypunct= separator wider than one byte is
returned as its first byte: under =fr_FR.UTF-8= =num_put<char>= writes
"1\xE2234\xE2567", invalid UTF-8; =fr_CA='s money separator is 0xC2.
=22.locale.numpunct= pins the first-byte reading. 14882:2003 makes the
separator one =char=. Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision: an ASCII fallback for a narrow separator that does
not fit (what glibc's =localeconv= returns is the datum), or record
the limit.

Check: the narrow output of every UTF-8 locale is valid UTF-8, or
the limit is in the master document.

** TODO A mixed-name global does not reach the C library

=locale::global= calls =setlocale= with the library's composite name,
which lists its six categories; glibc accepts a composite only when it
lists all twelve of its own, so =setlocale (LC_ALL, 0)= stays "C"
after =global= of a mixed-name locale (by probe). The comment at
=src/locale_global.cpp= 62-63 assumes =setlocale= understands the
format; =ref-locale.md= ch. 3 says the call takes effect. 14882:2003
22.1.1.5 p2. Found 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision: compose glibc's twelve-category name for the call,
or record that a mixed name does not reach the C library.

Check: =setlocale (LC_ALL, 0)= after =global= of a two-locale mixture
names both, or the master document records the limit.

** TODO The UTF-16 names set flags no converter reads

The constructor of =codecvt_byname<wchar_t, char>= (=src/wcodecvt.cpp=
near 1100) recognizes =UTF-16=, =UTF-16BE= and =UTF-16LE= and sets
=__rw_utf16*=, but no converter reads the flags: =out= under
=UTF-16= writes "61 c3 a9", UTF-8. Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision: implement the three, or stop recognizing the names
so the C library or =error= answers.

Check: per the decision; a row in =22.locale.codecvt= either way.

** TODO ISO-2022 conversion does not convert

By probe: =in= of "a ESC $ B $ \" ESC ( B" under =ISO-2022-JP=
returns =error= at byte 0; =out= of {a, U+3042} writes ESC $ @, the
1978 designation; =unshift= writes nothing; =-KR= and =-CN= answer
=error= and 0 to everything; =do_length= (=src/iso2022.cpp= 1871,
1876) returns =CODECVT_ERROR= (2) as a length when no state slot is
free, and a stray ~while (*from == ESCAPE_CHAR)~ around the escape
branch reads =*from= at =from_end= after an escape that ends the
input. No test covers any of it; the ISO-2022 branches also check no
state (=analysis-codecvt-invalid-state.md= ch. 0 says every path
does). Found 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision first: keep ISO-2022 and make it work against glibc's
=iconv=, or drop the four names.

Check: a round trip of the test fixtures through =iconv= and the
facet; ASan on =length= over "\x1b(B".

** TODO The =__rw_once= comment names a static mutex path that is gone

=src/once.cpp= 95-97 says the static mutex of =int= "is itself
constructed through =__rw_once= where mutexes cannot be initialized
statically". 92d18f73 removed that branch with
=_RWSTD_NO_STATIC_MUTEX_INIT=; =__rw_get_static_mutex= returns the
statically initialized mutex (=include/rw/_mutex.h= 193-198) and
calls no =__rw_once=.

Fix: the comment says what the code does.

Check: reading.

* Tests

The suite and its driver under =tests=: what it covers, what it
misses, and how it runs.

** TODO Two ctype test defects: a wrong character and an unbraced =TEST=

=tests/localization/22.locale.ctype.is.cpp= line 822 tests
=ct.widen ('\xa0')= for =DIGIT | GRAPH | PRINT= where
=22.locale.ctype.cpp= tests ='\xa2'=; the row passes only because =is=
matches any one bit. =22.locale.ctype.tolower.cpp= line 262 and
=22.locale.ctype.toupper.cpp= line 265 write ~if (sizeof (charT) > 1)
TEST (...)~ without braces, and =TEST= expands to two statements, so
the char pass asserts a stale =success=. Seen while adding the wide
rows beside them.

Fix: ='\xa2'= in the =is= row; braces around the two =TEST= calls.

Check: the =is= row stays green at the same count; =tolower= and
=toupper= each lose one assertion in the char pass; the six pins take
the new totals.

** TODO The era format comments of the =time_get= test are swapped

=struct time_data= in =tests/localization/22.locale.time.get.cpp=
labels =era_d_fmt= as =%EX= and =era_t_fmt= as =%Ex=; =era_d_fmt= is
the date, =%Ex=, and =era_t_fmt= the time, =%EX=. The initializer of
the generated locale labels its three formats right.

Fix: swap the two comments.

Check: none beyond the build; no assertion moves.

The =time_put= test has the same pair at
=tests/localization/22.locale.time.put.cpp= 316-317 (=era_d_fmt= labelled
=%EX=, =era_t_fmt= =%Ex=); found 2026-11-02.

** TODO The suite against other libraries

The suite tests the C++03 library through clauses 18 to 27. Most of
its assertions check standard behaviour, not the library's additions.
Run against libstdc++ and libc++, a disagreement is a defect in one of
the three or a question the standard leaves open.

Fix: build the driver and the tests of clauses 21 to 27 against
another library. The tests that need the library's additions or its
=_RWSTD_= macros are listed and left out, each with the reason.

Check: the census of the tests that build, a table per library, and
each disagreement read against the standard and filed where it
belongs.

** WAITING ThreadSanitizer cannot follow =longjmp= on glibc 2.44

Under the sanitizer every =longjmp= and =siglongjmp= dies with "can't
find longjmp buf" and exit 66. A ten-line program with =sigsetjmp=
and =siglongjmp= and no signal fails the same way, under GCC 16's
libtsan and Clang 23's runtime alike. The sanitizer records the stack
pointer at =setjmp= and, at =longjmp=, demangles the one saved in the
=jmp_buf= to find the record. glibc 2.44 (a5ec880f80, "Consolidate
pointer guard to use a relro variable instead of the TCB") moved the
x86-64 pointer guard from the thread control block slot at =%fs:0x30=
to the variable =__pointer_chk_guard=; the slot now reads zero, and
the runtimes still demangle with it. The same series gave aarch64 a
rotate its runtime does not undo (78f1f0e39c). Reported upstream as
LLVM issue 228263 (2026-10); GCC's libsanitizer takes the fix at its
next merge from compiler-rt. The six programs of the spot-check note
are the ones this stops.

Fix: the sanitizer runtimes'. The tests need no change.

Check: =0.alloc=, =0.new=, =0.inputiter=, =0.outputiter=,
=18.setjmp= and =18.csetjmp= exit 0 under a sanitizer runtime that
reads the new guard.

